AB  - This study aims to identify academic stress profiles among undergraduate engineering students, explore depression profile differences, and investigate whether stress perception mediates these differences, with psychological resilience as a moderator. Data were collected from 1,455 undergraduate engineering students using convenience sampling. Latent profile analysis and moderated mediation analysis were conducted. (1) Latent profile analysis revealed three academic stress profiles: Low research stress (62.0%), Low academic stress (17.4%), and High academic stress (20.6%). (2) Stress perception mediated the relationship between academic stress and depression, with academic stress positively predicting depression through stress perception. (3) Depression differences across the three profiles were significant, with psychological resilience moderating the effects. Specifically, resilience moderated the direct effect of the low research stress profile on depression (B = -0.07, SE = 0.03, p < 0.05) and the mediating effect of stress perception (B = 0.03, SE = 0.01, 95%CI [0.003, 0.05]). It also moderated the mediating effect of stress perception in the high academic stress profile (B = 0.16, SE = 0.02, 95%CI [0.12, 0.21]). The identification of three academic stress profiles offers insights for personalized interventions in engineering institutions. Stress perception and psychological resilience are crucial mediators and moderators of depression, highlighting the need for tailored mental health support for undergraduate engineering students.
